    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the number of ways to divide a day into \\( n \\) periods of \\( m \\) seconds, where \\( n \\) and \\( m \\) are positive integers, we need to consider the total number of seconds in a day and how they can be partitioned into smaller units.\n\nA day has \\( 24 \\) hours, and each hour has \\( 60 \\) minutes. Therefore, a day has:\n\\[\n24 \\text{ hours} \\times 60 \\text{ minutes/hour} \\times 60 \\text{ seconds/minute} = 86400 \\text{ seconds}\n\\]\n\nWe need to find all pairs \\((n, m)\\) such that:\n\\[\nn \\times m = 86400\n\\]\n\nEach pair \\((n, m)\\) represents a way to divide the day into \\( n \\) periods of \\( m \\) seconds. To find the total number of such pairs, we need to count the divisors of 86400.\n\nFirst, we find the prime factorization of 86400:\n\\[\n86400 \\div 2 = 43200\n\\]\n\\[\n43200 \\div 2 = 21600\n\\]\n\\[\n21600 \\div 2 = 10800\n\\]\n\\[\n10800 \\div 2 = 5400\n\\]\n\\[\n5400 \\div 2 = 2700\n\\]\n\\[\n2700 \\div 2 = 1350\n\\]\n\\[\n1350 \\div 2 = 675\n\\]\n\\[\n675 \\div 3 = 225\n\\]\n\\[\n225 \\div 3 = 75\n\\]\n\\[\n75 \\div 3 = 25\n\\]\n\\[\n25 \\div 5 = 5\n\\]\n\\[\n5 \\div 5 = 1\n\\]\n\nThus, the prime factorization of 86400 is:\n\\[\n86400 = 2^7 \\times 3^3 \\times 5^2\n\\]\n\nTo find the number of divisors, we use the formula for the number of divisors based on the prime factorization:\n\\[\n(n_1^{e_1} \\times n_2^{e_2} \\times \\cdots \\times n_k^{e_k}) = (e_1 + 1)(e_2 + 1) \\cdots (e_k + 1)\n\\]\n\nFor \\( 86400 = 2^7 \\times 3^3 \\times 5^2 \\), the number of divisors is:\n\\[\n(7 + 1)(3 + 1)(2 + 1) = 8 \\times 4 \\times 3 = 96\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the total number of ways to divide a day into \\( n \\) periods of \\( m \\) seconds, where \\( n \\) and \\( m \\) are positive integers, is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{96}\n\\]",
